					"uidString" : "BrxKTPnnT96FrTrzxw68eA=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "LanguageLink is how GT talks to Python, JavaScript, and a few other languages. To use it, you write an application, a subclass of {{gtClass:LanguageLinkApplication}}, and a server that speaks its protocol. If you need something to read along, {{gtClass:JSLinkApplication}} is complete and well-used, and I took a lot of inspiration from it."
			},
			{
				"__type" : "textSnippet",
				"children" : {
					"__type" : "snippets",
					"items" : [ ]
				},
				"createEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"createTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "BsNyOHdSSvWnDK+Yg5l2Nw=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "The first version of gt4carp went this way. Its application was very spartan: I tried to use stock LanguageLink classes wherever possible, and only added my own (an execution handler, a post-mortem debugger, and some settings) when that didn’t work. I didn’t write a server either! PythonBridge ships [a runtime](https://github.com/feenkcom/PythonBridge/tree/master/PythonBridgeRuntime) that includes all of the infrastructure, and the only part I had to change was how it handles `EvalCommand`s: mine fed the statements into a Carp REPL and read back whatever it printed."
			},
			{
				"__type" : "textSnippet",
				"children" : {
					"__type" : "snippets",
					"items" : [ ]
				},
				"createEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"createTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "v7Wfw02FR5mmJIYkxDDtfQ=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "That got me running code in a few afternoons, and if your language has a REPL, it might do the same for you. Just browse the existing implementors of each piece of infrastructure and “compare notes”. It will get you surprisingly far."
			},
			{
				"__type" : "textSnippet",
				"children" : {
					"__type" : "snippets",
					"items" : [ ]
				},
				"createEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"createTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "pYIITHzOSAuQnKEelmUxKA=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "Of course it had its limits, or it would still be here. The REPL answered with printed text, so every value came back as a string scraped from its output. And a REPL only knows how to run code. It can’t tell you what a form’s type is, where exactly an error happened, or what a macro expands to, and those were exactly the things I wanted the IDE to show. If you’re curious about the old code anyway, [this commit](https://github.com/carpentry-org/gt4carp/commit/9673c67) is where it went away."
			}
		]
	},
